Subject: Sign-extend signed bitfields in the generated accessors

A bitfield's bits arrive from its allocation unit zero-extended:
`__BindgenBitfieldUnit::get` and `get_const` both mask to the field's width and
hand the result back in a `u64`, and the getter casts that straight to the
field's declared type. The cast truncates but never extends, so a signed
bitfield narrower than its declared type reads back as its two's-complement bit
pattern: `int x : 3` holding -3 comes back as 5. `bool` and enum fields got arms
of their own when the accessors stopped transmuting (#3335); signed integers did
not. A signed field as wide as its type already reads correctly, because the
cast from the same-sized unsigned integer keeps every bit.

Extend before the cast: shift the value's top bit up to the sign bit of the
declared type, then arithmetic-shift it back. A field as wide as its declared
type shifts by zero, which is the same answer it gives today. A field declared
wider than its type is padding in the extra bits ([class.bit]/1), with nothing
above the type's own sign bit to extend from, so the shift saturates to zero
there too. The arithmetic shift happens in whatever `int_kind_rust_type` gives
that integer kind - the same route the rest of codegen takes to an integer
kind's Rust spelling - so it respects `--ctypes-prefix` and `--use-core` like
everything else. `char` is the one kind reachable here whose Rust spelling can
disagree with the IR about signedness, since `c_char` follows the Rust target
and `IntKind::Char` follows clang's; there the shift is logical, which is the
answer a getter returning an unsigned type wants anyway.

Widths above 64 are out of scope: `__BindgenBitfieldUnit` reads and writes
through a `u64` and asserts as much, so a bitfield wider than that is already
unsupported, sign or no sign.

Enum bitfields are left alone. They reach their type through `mem::transmute`
rather than a cast, and whether an enum's underlying type is signed is a
separate question from the field's.

Fixes #1160.

